<header><p class="st-kicker">princely state · Indian princely states</p><h1 class="st-title">Jaitpur State</h1><p class="st-meta">1731 CE – 1849 CE (approx.) · capital Jaitpur</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">A Bundela state of Bundelkhand formed from Chhatrasal's kingdom and annexed by the British under the doctrine of lapse in 1849.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>When <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chhatrasal">Maharaja Chhatrasal</a> died in 1731, his realm was divided between his sons and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peshwa">Peshwa</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jagat-raj">Jagat Raj</a> received the eastern share centred on Jaitpur, while his brother <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hirde-shah">Hirde Sah</a> held <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/panna-state">Panna</a>.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>The Jaitpur line held its lands through the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maratha-empire">Maratha</a> period and accepted British supremacy after 1803. Its Raja Parichhat was deposed in 1842 for backing the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bundelas">Bundela</a> rising, and a relative was installed in his place. Little else is recorded of the state's administration.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>When the installed ruler died without a natural heir in 1849, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lord-dalhousie">Lord Dalhousie</a> annexed Jaitpur under the doctrine of lapse, one of the first states taken that way. Its lands, around Jaitpur in today's <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mahoba">Mahoba</a> district of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/uttar-pradesh">Uttar Pradesh</a>, became British territory.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/jaitpur-state.jpg" alt="Jaitpur State" width="320" height="243"><figcaption>Image: John George Bartholomew (1860–1920) · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:JaitpurKart.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1731 CE – 1849 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/18th-century">18th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>princely state</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>feudal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Jaitpur</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Jagat Raj, Parichhat</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Hinduism</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Bundeli</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/india">India</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Jaitpur</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1790&amp;focus=jaitpur-state" aria-label="Jaitpur State on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="6790 1249 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Jaitpur State lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M7110 1505L7364 1601"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M7364 1601h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M7110 1505h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M7110 1505h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks its recorded location. <a href="/atlas?year=1790&amp;focus=jaitpur-state">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>

